<!-- INCIDENT LOG -->
<section class="logband rv" id="log"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Incident log</span><h2>The part nobody posts.</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">Anyone can publish a build that worked. These are real failures from my own environment: what I saw, what actually caused it, and what I changed. In two of the three, the root cause was not what the symptom pointed at.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ledger">
    <div class="ledger-top"><span>Selected incidents · 2026</span><span>Symptom · Root cause · Correction</span></div>
    <article class="entry">
      <div class="entry-meta"><div class="entry-no">01</div>17 AUG 2026<br>storage<br><span style="color:#4ED18C">resolved, 0 data loss</span></div>
      <div><div class="entry-t">Storage pool degraded, one disk faulted</div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk a">Symptom</span><p>RAIDZ1 dropped to <b>zero redundancy</b> after 189 read errors on a 4&nbsp;TB SAS drive. Every VM backup lives on that pool.</p></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk b">Root cause</span><p>Not the disk. All four drives had <b>zero grown defects</b> but were running 51 to 56&nbsp;°C against a 55&nbsp;°C trip point. The cage had no airflow, and SMART does not traverse virtio, so nothing was watching temperature.</p></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk c">Correction</span><p>Cooled the cage, watched all four drop 8&nbsp;°C, then cleared the fault. Resilvered in <b>28 seconds</b>. No replacement drive, no data lost.</p></div>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry">
      <div class="entry-meta"><div class="entry-no">02</div>07 AUG 2026<br>kernel<br><span style="color:#4ED18C">resolved</span></div>
      <div><div class="entry-t">Two unrelated services failing for one reason</div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk a">Symptom</span><p>The SIEM reported <b>no API available</b> and every agent showed disconnected. Separately, the log collector could not start a file watcher.</p></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk b">Root cause</span><p>A single kernel setting. <b>fs.inotify.max_user_instances</b> was still at its default of 128 and had been exhausted, taking down two systems that looked unconnected.</p></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk c">Correction</span><p>Raised and persisted the limit across all three nodes. Both services recovered without touching anything else.</p></div>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry">
      <div class="entry-meta"><div class="entry-no">03</div>04 AUG 2026<br>availability<br><span style="color:#4ED18C">resolved, prevented</span></div>
      <div><div class="entry-t">Control plane node unreachable for 23 hours</div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk a">Symptom</span><p>The node answered nothing on the network, yet a cron job on it kept writing to a log file the entire time.</p></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk b">Root cause</span><p>An unattended upgrade triggered a reboot that <b>hung mid shutdown</b>. systemd had already torn down every mount and network unit, so the machine was alive but deaf.</p></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="rk c">Correction</span><p>Disabled automatic reboots and capped the unit stop timeout, so a stuck service can no longer stall a shutdown indefinitely.</p></div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</div></section>

