<section class="sec rv"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Straight answers</span><h2>The questions you are already asking.</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">If you are weighing someone without professional IT experience, these are probably the objections in your head. Here they are, answered directly.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <details open><summary>Why hire someone with no professional IT experience?</summary>
      <p>You should weigh that seriously. What I offer instead is evidence: an environment I designed and operate, a written record of failures I diagnosed with nobody to escalate to, and the habit of documenting what I changed and why. I am applying for entry level roles because that is what I am. But I will not be starting from zero on day one.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is this really yours, or did you follow a guide?</summary>
      <p>Mine. Every screenshot comes out of my own environment, and every write-up includes what went wrong, which is the tell, because tutorials never do. The incident log above is the clearest proof. Those root causes took days to find and none of them were what the symptom suggested.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What are you actually looking for?</summary>
      <p>A first IT role in the San Antonio area: help desk, systems administration, or infrastructure support. I live in Texas and am open to hybrid or on site. I work nights in a hospital lab now, so I am used to being the person who is there when something breaks at three in the morning.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does the Navy background have to do with it?</summary>
      <p>I was a Gas Turbine Systems Technician (Electrical) in Yokosuka, Japan from 2009 to 2013, standing watch on a warship's electrical plant and maintaining propulsion control systems and alarm circuitry. That is where I learned to isolate a fault methodically instead of guessing, and to accept that the obvious explanation is usually wrong. It is the most transferable thing I brought with me.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How is this site served?</summary>
      <p>From the cluster described above. A tag push builds a hardened non root container, pushes it to a registry, and rolls it out in about forty seconds end to end. It runs active active from my homelab and an ARM cluster in Oracle Cloud, reached through Cloudflare Tunnels with no inbound ports open on my network.</p></details>
  </div>
</div></section>

